[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [{:db/id 17592186068807 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1fd4a51b-43a7-5d87-a486-e59b745ec11b" "question-a5621f27-e279-5ddb-b2e9-fbd134010344"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068650 :question/problem "![](images/06/06a54157969bfc559c8622512bca59b796f8100f8b52542165acb1eb2f397585.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}{:}$\n\n- The point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph.\n\n- Its domain is $x\\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\n- Its range is $y\\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\n- The graph lies in the first and third quadrants.\n\n- The function is increasing throughout its entire domain.\n\n- As $x \\rightarrow \\infty,$ we have $y\\rightarrow \\infty,$ though the function increases more slowly as $x$ gets large.\n\n- As $x \\rightarrow -\\infty,$ we have $y\\rightarrow -\\infty,$  though the function decreases more slowly as $x$ becomes very negative.\n\nOf the given options, only graph I has all of the above properties. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068808 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-98e4ebcc-8114-515c-b8a9-5feafd8f7bb3" "question-90b71102-b1fe-54a8-9326-1ad18a9d698b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068651 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x) =\\sqrt[3]{x}?$\n\nI. The point $(64,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$\n\nII. The domain of $f(x)$ is $x\\in[-1,\\infty)$\n\nIII. $f(x) \\rightarrow -\\infty$ as $x \\rightarrow -\\infty$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}.$\n\n![](images/ab/abafb7d55b4baa0b06f20c3de2b44d5925f7286f078aaa76d71aded7c9c9a001.png)\n\nNow, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(64)=\\sqrt[3]{64}=4.$\n\n- Statement II is false since $f(x) =\\sqrt[3]{x}$ is defined for $x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $f(x) \\rightarrow -\\infty$ as $x \\rightarrow -\\infty.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-1fd4a51b-43a7-5d87-a486-e59b745ec11b" :question/id #uuid "1fd4a51b-43a7-5d87-a486-e59b745ec11b" :question/math-academy-id "q-89848" :question/problem "![](images/49/496cfd2dc0864e509a1f3f958f2578cbe70684a19b93aecf89d6b38b8c7f5680.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}.$\n\n- The point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph\n\n- Its domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n\n- Its range is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n\n- The graph lies in the first and third quadrants\n\n- The function is increasing throughout its entire domain.\n\n- As $x\\to \\infty $, we have $y\\to \\infty ,$ though it does so slowly\n\n- As $x\\to -\\infty $, we have $y\\to -\\infty ,$ though it does so slowly\n\nOf the given options, only graph III has all of the above properties. Therefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cf642bc1-70e2-52da-8620-796b98152ad1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cf642bc1-70e2-52da-8620-796b98152ad1" :answer-field/id #uuid "cf642bc1-70e2-52da-8620-796b98152ad1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b0a00124-75cf-5a09-a7f0-5ba217241247" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ed0fc472-5950-59ab-96b3-5d270c258ab9" "answer-1e3d5772-564e-5836-bedd-d69ef7b718a1" "answer-8932775f-ab39-5fb4-80b2-054154f83d12" "answer-05916394-c595-5968-81f7-35a5b3795571" "answer-b0a00124-75cf-5a09-a7f0-5ba217241247"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ed0fc472-5950-59ab-96b3-5d270c258ab9" :answer/id #uuid "ed0fc472-5950-59ab-96b3-5d270c258ab9" :answer/type 