I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-532a1303-6f64-5e49-974c-c40278d75b29" :answer/id #uuid "532a1303-6f64-5e49-974c-c40278d75b29" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-52251806-d785-5838-a46d-8bf20af66d4a" :answer/id #uuid "52251806-d785-5838-a46d-8bf20af66d4a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b60038db-477f-5ef7-9af4-1e5925f39ccc" :answer/id #uuid "b60038db-477f-5ef7-9af4-1e5925f39ccc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-09fca4ca-1aba-590b-8f95-45dedb57d0bd" :answer/id #uuid "09fca4ca-1aba-590b-8f95-45dedb57d0bd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186069984 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to plot the graph of $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}.$  First, we create a table of values.\n\n| $x$ | $-8$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $f(x)$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nNotice that we included both positive and negative input values since the cube root is defined over all real numbers.\n\nNow, we can plot the graph:\n\n![](images/06/06fb1e18af5c6069d8d0ca0d4ac2e7c3d8103f7b3e672091d05727323ff0d248.png)\n\nSome key points to note:\n\n- The graph passes through the points $(0,0)$ and $(1,1).$\n\n- Its domain is $x\\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\n- Its range is $y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\n- The function is increasing throughout its entire domain.\n\n- As $x \\rightarrow \\infty,$ we have $y\\rightarrow \\infty,$ though the function increases more slowly as $x$ gets large.\n\n- As $x \\rightarrow -\\infty,$ we have $y\\rightarrow -\\infty,$  though the function decreases more slowly as $x$ becomes very negative.\n\nIt is also worth noting that $f(x) =\\sqrt[3]{x}$ is an invertible function, and its inverse is\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = x^3, \\qquad x\\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nThe graph of $f(x) = \\sqrt[3]{x}$ and its inverse are shown below.\n\n![](images/0c/0cb78384fd1ce682758df76ac9b014b105de080b5923df2b313598b8fc7eae46.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068981 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}] "assertions" [[17592186068650 :question/problem "![](images/06/06a54157969bfc559c8622512bca59b796f8100f8b52542165acb1eb2f397585.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}?$"] [17592186068650 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}{:}$\n\n- The point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph.\n\n- Its domain is $x\\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\n- Its range is $y\\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\n- The graph lies in the first and third quadrants.\n\n- The function is increasing throughout its entire domain.\n\n- As $x \\rightarrow \\infty,$ we have $y\\rightarrow \\infty,$ though the function increases more slowly as $x$ gets large.\n\n- As $x \\rightarrow -\\infty,$ we have $y\\rightarrow -\\infty,$  though the function decreases more slowly as $x$ becomes very negative.\n\nOf the given options, only graph I has all of the above properties. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186068651 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x) =\\sqrt[3]{x}?$\n\nI. The point $(64,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$\n\nII. The domain of $f(x)$ is $x\\in[-1,\\infty)$\n\nIII. $f(x) \\rightarrow -\\infty$ as $x \\rightarrow -\\infty$"] [17592186068651 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}.$\n\n![](images/ab/abafb7d55b4baa0b06f20c3de2b44d5925f7286f078aaa76d71aded7c9c9a001.png)\n\nNow, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(64)=\\sqrt[3]{64}=4.$\n\n- Statement II is false since $f(x) =\\sqrt[3]{x}$ is defined for $x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $f(x) \\rightarrow -\\infty$ as $x \\rightarrow -\\infty.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] ["question-1fd4a51b-43a7-5d87-a486-e59b745ec11b" :question/id #uuid "1fd4a51b-43a7-5d87-a486-e59b745ec11b"] ["question-1fd4a51b-43a7-5d87-a486-e59b745ec11b" :question/math-academy-id "q-89848"] ["question-1fd4a51b-43a7-5d87-a486-e59b745ec11b" :question/problem "![](images/49/496cfd2dc0864e509a1f3f958f2578cbe70684a19b93aecf89d6b38b8c7f5680.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}?$"] ["question-1fd4a51b-43a7-5d87-a486-e59b745ec11b" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}.$\n\n- The point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph\n\n- Its domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n\n- Its range is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n\n- The graph lies in the first and third quadrants\n\n- The function is increasing throughout its entire domain.\n\n- As $x\\to \\infty $, we have $y\\to \\infty ,$ though it does so slowly\n\n- As $x\\to -\\infty $, we have $y\\to -\\infty ,$ though it does so slowly\n\nOf the given options, only graph III has all of the above properties. Therefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""] ["question-1fd4a51b-43a7-5d87-a486-e59b745ec11b" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-field-cf642bc1-70e2-52da-8620-796b98152ad1" :answer-field/id #uuid "cf642bc1-70e2-52da-8620-796b98152ad1"] ["answer-ed0fc472-5950-59ab-96b3-5d270c258ab9" :answer/id #uuid "ed0fc472-5950-59ab-96b3-5d270c258ab9"] ["answer-ed0fc472-5950-59ab-96b3-5d270c258ab9" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-ed0fc472-5950-59ab-96b3-5d270c258ab9" :answer/value "I and III only"] ["answer-1e3d5772-564e-5836-bedd-d69ef7b718a1" :answer/id #uuid "1e3d5772-564e-5836-bedd-d69ef7b718a1"] ["answer-1e3d5772-564e-5836-bedd-d69ef7b718a1" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-1e3d5772-564e-5836-bedd-d69ef7b718a1" :answer/value "I and IV only"] ["answer-8932775f-ab39-5fb4-80b2-054154f83d12" :answer/id #uuid "8932775f-ab39-5fb