\n\nNow, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since the range of $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $f(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is false since the graph of $f(x)$ lies in the first and third quadrants\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] ["question-90b71102-b1fe-54a8-9326-1ad18a9d698b" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-f2080000-1873-59da-afbb-0f81bfffa11d" :answer-field/id #uuid "f2080000-1873-59da-afbb-0f81bfffa11d"] ["answer-d6785992-fea4-51e7-ba38-0c8c87bf84ee" :answer/id #uuid "d6785992-fea4-51e7-ba38-0c8c87bf84ee"] ["answer-d6785992-fea4-51e7-ba38-0c8c87bf84ee" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-d6785992-fea4-51e7-ba38-0c8c87bf84ee" :answer/value "I and II only"] ["answer-532a1303-6f64-5e49-974c-c40278d75b29" :answer/id #uuid "532a1303-6f64-5e49-974c-c40278d75b29"] ["answer-532a1303-6f64-5e49-974c-c40278d75b29" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-532a1303-6f64-5e49-974c-c40278d75b29" :answer/value "I only"] ["answer-52251806-d785-5838-a46d-8bf20af66d4a" :answer/id #uuid "52251806-d785-5838-a46d-8bf20af66d4a"] ["answer-52251806-d785-5838-a46d-8bf20af66d4a" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-52251806-d785-5838-a46d-8bf20af66d4a" :answer/value "II and III only"] ["answer-b60038db-477f-5ef7-9af4-1e5925f39ccc" :answer/id #uuid "b60038db-477f-5ef7-9af4-1e5925f39ccc"] ["answer-b60038db-477f-5ef7-9af4-1e5925f39ccc" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-b60038db-477f-5ef7-9af4-1e5925f39ccc" :answer/value "II only"] ["answer-09fca4ca-1aba-590b-8f95-45dedb57d0bd" :answer/id #uuid "09fca4ca-1aba-590b-8f95-45dedb57d0bd"] ["answer-09fca4ca-1aba-590b-8f95-45dedb57d0bd" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-09fca4ca-1aba-590b-8f95-45dedb57d0bd" :answer/value "III only"] ["answer-field-f2080000-1873-59da-afbb-0f81bfffa11d" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-f2080000-1873-59da-afbb-0f81bfffa11d" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-f2080000-1873-59da-afbb-0f81bfffa11d" :answer-field/correct "answer-b60038db-477f-5ef7-9af4-1e5925f39ccc"] ["answer-field-f2080000-1873-59da-afbb-0f81bfffa11d" :answer-field/choices "answer-d6785992-fea4-51e7-ba38-0c8c87bf84ee"] ["answer-field-f2080000-1873-59da-afbb-0f81bfffa11d" :answer-field/choices "answer-532a1303-6f64-5e49-974c-c40278d75b29"] ["answer-field-f2080000-1873-59da-afbb-0f81bfffa11d" :answer-field/choices "answer-52251806-d785-5838-a46d-8bf20af66d4a"] ["answer-field-f2080000-1873-59da-afbb-0f81bfffa11d" :answer-field/choices "answer-b60038db-477f-5ef7-9af4-1e5925f39ccc"] ["answer-field-f2080000-1873-59da-afbb-0f81bfffa11d" :answer-field/choices "answer-09fca4ca-1aba-590b-8f95-45dedb57d0bd"] ["question-90b71102-b1fe-54a8-9326-1ad18a9d698b" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-f2080000-1873-59da-afbb-0f81bfffa11d"] [17592186068808 :knowledge-point/questions "question-90b71102-b1fe-54a8-9326-1ad18a9d698b"] [17592186069984 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to plot the graph of $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}.$  First, we create a table of values.\n\n| $x$ | $-8$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $f(x)$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nNotice that we included both positive and negative input values since the cube root is defined over all real numbers.\n\nNow, we can plot the graph:\n\n![](images/06/06fb1e18af5c6069d8d0ca0d4ac2e7c3d8103f7b3e672091d05727323ff0d248.png)\n\nSome key points to note:\n\n- The graph passes through the points $(0,0)$ and $(1,1).$\n\n- Its domain is $x\\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\n- Its range is $y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\n- The function is increasing throughout its entire domain.\n\n- As $x \\rightarrow \\infty,$ we have $y\\rightarrow \\infty,$ though the function increases more slowly as $x$ gets large.\n\n- As $x \\rightarrow -\\infty,$ we have $y\\rightarrow -\\infty,$  though the function decreases more slowly as $x$ becomes very negative.\n\nIt is also worth noting that $f(x) =\\sqrt[3]{x}$ is an invertible function, and its inverse is\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = x^3, \\qquad x\\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nThe graph of $f(x) = \\sqrt[3]{x}$ and its inverse are shown below.\n\n![](images/0c/0cb78384fd1ce682758df76ac9b014b105de080b5923df2b313598b8fc7eae46.png)"] [17592186068981 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0]] "retractions" [[17592186068650 :question/problem "![](images/06/06a54157969bfc559c8622512bca59b796f8100f8b52542165acb1eb2f397585.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}$?"] [17592186068650 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall some of the basic properties of\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt[3]{x}{:}\n$$\n\n- The point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph.\n- Its domain is $x\\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n- Its range is $y\\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n- The graph lies in the first and third quadrants.\n- The function is increasing throughout its entire domain.\n- As $x \\rightarrow \\infty$, we have $y\\rightarrow \\infty$, though the function increases more slowly as $x$ gets large.\n- As $x \\rightarrow -\\infty$, we have $y\\rightarrow -\\infty$, though the function decreases more slowly as $x$ becomes very negative.\n\nOf the given options, only graph I has all of the above properties. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186068651 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x) =\\sqrt[3]{x}$?\n\n1. The point $(64,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$\n2. The domain of $f(x)$ is $x\\in[-1,\\infty)$\n3. $f(x) \\rightarrow -\\infty$ as $x \\rightarrow -\\infty$"] [17592186068651 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt[3]{x}\n$$\n\n![](images/ab/abafb7d55b4baa0b06f20c3de2b44d5925f7286f078aaa76d71aded7c9c9a001.png)\n\nNow, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(64)=\\sqrt[3]{64}=4$.\n- Statement II is false since $f(x) =\\sqrt[3]{x}$ is defined for $x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$.\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $f(x) \\rightarrow -\\infty$ as $x \\rightarrow -\\infty$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [17592186069984 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to plot the graph of\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we create a table of values.\n\n| $x$ | $-8$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $8$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nNotice that we included both positive and negative input values since the cube root is defined over all real numbers.\n\nNow, we can plot the graph:\n\n![](images/06/06fb1e18af5c6069d8d0ca0d4ac2e7c3d8103f7b3e672091d05727323ff0d248.png)\n\nSome key points to note:\n\n- The graph passes through the points $(0,0)$ and $(1,1)$.\n- Its domain is $x\\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n- Its range is $y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n- The function is increasing throughout its entire domain.\n- As $x \\rightarrow \\infty$, we have $y\\rightarrow \\infty$, though the function increases more slowly as $x$ gets large.\n- As $x \\rightarrow -\\infty$, we have $y\\rightarrow -\\infty$, though the function decreases more slowly as $x$ becomes very negative.\n\nIt is also worth noting that\n\n$$\nf(x) =\\sqrt[3]{x}\n$$\n\nis an invertible function, and its inverse is\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = x^3, \\qquad x\\in (-\\infty,\\infty)\n$$\n\nThe graph of\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\sqrt[3]{x}\n$$\n\nand its inverse are shown below.\n\n![](images/0c/0cb78384fd1ce682758df76ac9b014b105de080b5923df2b313598b8fc7eae46.png)"]]}]